<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063T">Example <span data-tag="063T">9.3.1.9</span>.</a> Following the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, a cardinal $\kappa $ is small if it satisfies $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $, where $ \Omega $ is some fixed strongly inaccessible cardinal. In this case, a functor of $\infty $-categories $H: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ exhibits $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="063N" href="/tag/063N">9.3.1.4</a>) if and only if it exhibits $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{ \Omega }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="063R" href="/tag/063R">9.3.1.7</a>). In particular, $H$ exhibits $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ if and only if it exhibits $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\aleph _0}^{ \Omega }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Stated more informally, we have equivalences </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \simeq \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{ \Omega }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \quad \quad \operatorname{Ind}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \simeq \operatorname{Ind}_{\aleph _0}^{ \Omega }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}). \]
</div>
</article>
